# =============================================================================
# DeepSeek-V4-Flash MXFP4 at 524288 context -- KV in RAM, experts in VRAM
# =============================================================================
# The model trains to 1 048 576 (yarn x16 over 65 536), so 512k is in range.
# What makes it usable is an inverted placement, measured in RESULTS.md §10:
#
#   At 512k the MLA KV wants 21.5 GiB of VRAM, and every GiB of KV is a GiB of
#   experts pushed out to DDR5 -- which is what actually caps generation. So put
#   the KV in RAM (-nkvo) and spend the VRAM on experts instead. DeepSeek Sparse
#   Attention attends only ~512 positions no matter how deep the context is, so
#   a KV in slow memory costs almost nothing (2 %), while 29 GiB of experts
#   moving out of DDR5 is worth a lot.
#
#   MEASURED at 130k depth, 512k context (2026-08, -ub 512):
#     --fit baseline        55.4 GiB GPU / 90.2 DDR5   205.5 pp / 13.33 tg
#     this profile          84.1 GiB GPU / 61.6 DDR5   277.9 pp / 16.30 tg
#   i.e. +35 % prefill, +22 % generation, and RAM use is unchanged (the KV
#   arriving is offset by the experts leaving).
#
#   As shipped now (-ncmoe 21, -ub 8192, the mask patch, 600 W; RESULTS §49.13):
#     32k    1 965 pp / 18.39 tg
#     128k   1 741 pp / 17.61 tg      (the 128k default profile: 1 711 / 18.99)
#     256k   1 359 pp / 16.80 tg
#     500k     806 pp / 15.64 tg      (499 951 tokens: 10.3 min of prefill,
#                                      where the August profile needed ~52)
#   At the 400 W cap this machine normally runs, prefill is 5-7 % lower and
#   generation the same.
#
#   The prompt cache is off (below), so every turn re-prefills. 512k is for
#   long single shots, not chat.
#
#   ./serve.sh deepseek-v4-flash-512k
#   ./serve-deepseek-v4-flash-mxfp4-gpu-experts-512k.sh
# -----------------------------------------------------------------------------

# Same MXFP4 quant as the deepseek-v4-flash profile (~145.6 GiB, 4-file split).
: "${IK_MODEL_GLOB:=${IK_MODELS_ROOT}/lmstudio-community/DeepSeek-V4-Flash-0731-GGUF/DeepSeek-V4-Flash-0731-MXFP4-00001-of-*.gguf}"

: "${IK_ALIAS:=deepseek-v4-flash-512k}"
: "${IK_CTX:=524288}"

# --- placement: MANUAL, --fit is actively wrong here ------------------------
# --fit subtracts the KV size from the VRAM it thinks it has even when -nkvo
# means the KV is never allocated there, so `-nkvo` under --fit leaves ~31 GiB
# of VRAM idle and the weight split byte-identical to not using it at all
# (VERIFIED: 92 402 MiB CPU / 56 726 MiB GPU with and without). Hence IK_FIT=0
# and an explicit split.
#
# 19 is the ceiling at -ub 512: --n-cpu-moe 17 CUDA-OOMs on the 13.2 GiB compute
# buffer, and a smaller -ub does not rescue it (RESULTS §10.3 -- 512 -> 256
# frees only 668 MiB and costs 21 % prefill).
: "${IK_FIT:=0}"
# 21 since 2026-09-04 (RESULTS §49.13). It was 25, and 25 was the floor: at
# 524288 the old graph's CUDA compute buffer was 21 376 MiB at -ub 8192 --
# three times the 131072 figure, because it held one mask copy per GPU expert
# layer, each sized by the CONTEXT (§29, §49.9). n24 loaded and died at 32k.
# With the mask-share patch the buffer is 7 304 MiB, and the 14 GiB it freed
# went back into expert layers: 21 peaks at 93 054 MiB of 97 887 from the
# first u-batch to 256k depth and 93 706 at 500k, 4.2 GiB free (more than 25
# had on the old graph). Each layer moved to the GPU was worth ~3 % of
# generation. 20 would leave ~1.6 GiB; not tried. Without the patch (IK_MASK_SHARE=0) this
# placement does not fit -- set IK_NCMOE=25 with it.
: "${IK_NCMOE:=21}"

# f16, not q8_0: the MLA cache is an already-compressed latent. Quantizing it
# does free VRAM, but --fit then needs a margin big enough for the compute
# buffer and gives most of the gain straight back (net +2 %, RESULTS §10.2).
: "${IK_CTK:=f16}"
: "${IK_CTV:=f16}"

# 8192 rather than the old 512. On the old graph the two levers conflicted at
# this context -- a bigger micro-batch needed a bigger buffer, paid for with
# expert layers in host RAM -- and both sides were measured (§29.2):
#
#   n25 / ub 8192   1069 / 1721 / 1335 pp    16.91 / 16.26 / 14.86 tg
#   n22 / ub 4096   1112 / 1454 / 1141 pp    18.54 / 17.71 / 16.06 tg
#
# and the batch won: on a 512k prompt the prefill difference was 66 seconds,
# the generation difference on a 500-token answer 2.5. With the patched buffer
# the conflict is gone -- 8192 AND n21 fit (§49.13) -- so there is nothing to
# trade any more.
: "${IK_UBATCH:=8192}"

# -b MUST be >= -ub or the micro-batch is silently clamped down to it. This
# profile had no IK_BATCH line at all, so -b came from default.env as 4096 and
# quietly turned -ub 8192 into 4096 -- worth ~20 % of prefill at depth, and it
# cost one wasted sweep on 2026-08-19 before the mismatch was spotted.
: "${IK_BATCH:=8192}"

# --- context state: off, deliberately ---------------------------------------
# -ctx-ckpt 0    the 32 per-slot context checkpoints and
# --cache-ram 0  the 8 GiB prompt cache
# cost 54 GiB of RAM and 26 % of generation at this class of context (§9.3), and
# a 250k prefill with them on was OOM-killed at 203 GiB anon-rss. The trade is
# real though: with the prompt cache off, re-sending a conversation re-prefills
# it from scratch, which at 512k is ten minutes (§49.13). This profile is for
# long single-shot contexts, not multi-turn chat.
: "${IK_EXTRA_ARGS:=-mla 3 -fidx --reasoning-format deepseek -nkvo -ctx-ckpt 0 --cache-ram 0}"
